.TH V32ASLD 1 "2026-11-04" "v32asld 20261101-dev" "Vircon32 assembler manual"
.SH NAME
v32asld \- linker and archiver for v32as relocatable objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32asld
.RI [ options ]
.IR OBJECT ...
.br
.B v32asld \-A
.I ARCHIVE
.IR OBJECT ...
.br
.B v32asld \-R
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.IR OBJECT ...
.br
.B v32asld \-\-v32x \-\-data
.I WORDS
.RI [ options ]
.IR OBJECT ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32asld
links relocatable objects made by
.B v32as \-C
(see
.BR v32as (1))
into a VBIN file.
Linking is exactly equivalent to assembling the objects' sources one after the
other as a single file at the chosen base address; the test suite checks this
byte for byte.
.PP
Objects are placed in command line order, and the first word of the first
object is the entry point (see
.BR \-\-entry ).
Symbols declared
.B global
in one object satisfy the
.B extern
references of the others.
A label that is neither is local to its object, so two objects may use the same
local name.
The objects carry the debug records of the assembler, so
.B \-g
writes the same
.I .debug
file that
.B v32as \-g
writes for the sources joined together.
.PP
Archives (files starting with the magic
.BR V32\-VARC ,
made with
.BR \-A )
may be named as operands or found with
.BR \-l .
They are read after all plain objects: a member is linked in (appended, in the
order found) only when it defines a global symbol that some selected object
still lacks, and the scan repeats until nothing more is pulled in.
.PP
The object and archive formats are described in
.I docs/OBJECT.md
in the source distribution.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output =\fIFILE\fP
Output file.
The default is
.I a.vbin
(or
.I a.v32ob
with
.BR \-R ).
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bios
Link for the BIOS: addresses start at 0x10000000.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cart
Link for a cartridge: addresses start at 0x20000000.
This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-origin " \fIADDR\fP"
Link for any base address.
.I ADDR
is read as a C integer and must fit in 32 bits.
.BR \-b ", " \-c " and " \-O
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-debug [= \fIMODE\fP]
Also write
.IR OUTPUT .debug
as
.B v32as \-g
does.
.I MODE
is
.B program
(the default; absolute word addresses),
.B vbin
or
.B v32
(byte offsets in a .vbin or .v32 file); it may also be given as the next word.
The labels written are every label of every object, local or global; the lines
are those of the sources (or the C lines, when an object was made with
.BR "v32as \-C \-\-map" ).
If an object has
.B ;@var
records,
.IR OUTPUT .vars
is written as well, as
.B v32as \-g
would for the joined source.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbols= FILE
Write
.I 0xADDRESS,name,kind
for every label of every object, sorted by address.
.I kind
is
.B F
(code follows) or
.B D
(data).
Local labels appear under the name the assembler gave them (for example
.BR _f__loop ).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-relocs =\fIFILE\fP , " \-\-emit\-relocs=" \fIFILE\fP
Write
.I index,0xVALUE[,kind]
for every word that holds an address, in the format of
.BR "v32as \-r" :
the zero\-based word index into the image (not counting the 3\-word VBIN
header), the value as linked, and, when any object declares a
.BR datasym ,
the kind
.B C
(code address) or
.B D
(data address) on every line.
.TP
.BI \-\-entry= SYMBOL
The program starts at the global label
.IR SYMBOL .
Unless it is the first word of the first object, a two\-word
.B jmp SYMBOL
is placed in front of everything (it appears in the
.B \-r
file).
It is an error if no object defines
.IR SYMBOL .
.TP
.B \-\-v32x
Write a V32X executable for v32nix instead of a VBIN: an 8\-word header
(0x58323356, version 1, 8, text words, data words, entry offset, origin, 0)
followed by the image, which is the same as what
.B v32as \-\-origin
makes.
Needs
.BR \-\-data .
The base defaults to 0x100000 (the v32nix window base) and is the origin
recorded in the header;
.B \-O
changes it, and
.BR \-b ,
.B \-c
and
.B "\-g vbin"
or
.B "\-g v32"
are refused.
With
.B \-\-v32x ,
.B \-\-entry
is a global label or a word offset into the image (default 0) that goes into the
header; no jump is added, and it must lie inside the image.
.TP
.BI \-\-data= WORDS
With
.BR \-\-v32x :
the number of zeroed global\-variable words behind the image.
It is an error without
.BR \-\-v32x ,
and
.B \-\-v32x
needs it.
.TP
.BI \-L " DIR"
Add
.I DIR
to the directories searched by
.BR \-l .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-l " NAME"
Read the archive
.IR DIR /\fINAME\fP.v32a
or else
.IR DIR /lib\fINAME\fP.a
from the first of the
.B \-L
directories that has one; the current directory is always searched last.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-archive =\fIFILE\fP
Do not link: make an archive
.I FILE
(format
.BR V32\-VARC )
of the objects given.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-relocatable
Partial link: do not link, but join the objects into one object
(default output
.IR a.v32ob ).
Globals stay global, an
.B extern
that another input defines becomes a reference to the definition, and the others
stay
.BR extern ;
relocations, datasyms and debug records are kept, so linking the result is the
same as linking the inputs.
Archives and
.B \-l
work as in a full link.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-O ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-\-symbols ,
.B \-\-entry
or
.BR \-\-v32x .
Note that
.B \-r
is the relocation list, not a relocatable link.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no\-warnings
Do not warn about an
.B extern
declaration that no code uses.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A link error: an undefined symbol (reported once per symbol, as
.B "undefined reference to `x' (first used in a.v32ob)" ),
a global defined by two objects, an entry symbol that is not defined or lies
outside the image.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, an unreadable or corrupt object or archive (not a v32as object,
truncated, or an unsupported format version), a missing
.B \-l
library, or an output file that cannot be written.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B v32asld
reads no environment variables.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I a.vbin
Default linked output.
.TP
.I a.v32ob
Default output of
.BR \-R .
.TP
.IR NAME .v32a ", " libNAME .a
Archives searched for by
.BR \-l .
.SH EXAMPLES
Two sources, assembled separately and linked:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-C a.asm
v32as \-C b.asm \-o b.v32ob
v32asld \-g \-r prog.rel \-\-symbols prog.sym \-o prog.vbin a.v32ob b.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.PP
Here
.I a.asm
has
.B global _start
and
.BR "extern _helper" ,
and
.I b.asm
has
.BR "global _helper" .
The result is identical to assembling the two sources joined into one.
.PP
Make an archive and link against it, by name and with
.BR \-l :
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32asld \-A libb.a b.v32ob
v32asld \-o prog.vbin a.v32ob libb.a
v32asld \-o prog.vbin \-L libs \-l b a.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.PP
Start at a label that is not the first word (a
.B jmp
is put in front):
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32asld \-\-entry _helper \-o prog.vbin a.v32ob b.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.PP
Join two objects into one, then link it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32asld \-R \-o ab.v32ob a.v32ob b.v32ob
v32asld \-o prog.vbin ab.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.PP
A V32X executable with 4 words of zeroed data, starting at
.BR _helper :
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32asld \-\-v32x \-\-data 4 \-\-entry _helper \-o prog.v32x a.v32ob b.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages start with
.BR v32asld: .
Besides the errors above, a warning is given for an
.B extern
declaration that is never used (silenced by
.BR \-w ).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR v32as (1),
.BR v32as\-language (7)
.PP
docs/OBJECT.md in the source distribution describes the object and archive
formats.
